  *
+ * Zero-copy scanout (patch 0234, boot-arg negronifb_zerocopy=1, default 0):
+ * the display engine (SM8450 SDE) fetches backboardd's framebuffers itself.
+ * start() reads the engine's state (read only, always; boot-arg
+ * negronifb_dpuprobe=0 skips it) and finds the source pipe(s) whose address
+ * is the panel memory (ABL's splash layout). When the layout is the expected
+ * one, the rest of ABL's splash region becomes display slots that
+ * NegroniSurface.cpp gives backboardd's canvas-sized BGRA framebuffers. A swap
+ * of one such framebuffer alone, full canvas, is then shown by pointing the
+ * pipes at it (source address, stride, crop offset of the centred panel part)
+ * and a CTL flush: the engine takes the new address at its next frame start
+ * (the panel's 120 Hz refresh), so nothing tears and no CPU or GPU copies the
+ * frame. A GPU-rendered one is flipped once its fence is done (checked every
+ * vsync tick, nobody waits); for a CPU-rendered one the rows CoreAnimation
+ * wrote (the swaps' dirty regions) are cleaned from the CPU caches first. The
+ * shown and the pending framebuffer stay held in use; SwapWait (selector 6)
+ * waits (at most 50 ms) until the swap it names is on screen, as the S9 port
+ * (external/ios-s9 0096) does. The clock shift is done in the framebuffer
+ * itself while it is shown and undone before CoreAnimation gets it back. Any
+ * other frame takes the copy paths above, and the pipes go back to the panel
+ * memory once it holds that frame; display off and a panic do that too. No
+ * zero-copy with negronibadge=1, with the console drawn over the GUI
+ * (negronifb_noconsole=0) or with negronifb_shift.
+ *
  * The panel memory itself is published as an IOSurface and returned as the
